\details{
Supply a list of three formulae with the response in the first.
The identity link models the \code{alpha} quantile. A log link models the
spread, the difference between the \code{1 - beta / 2} and \code{beta / 2}
quantiles. A second log link models the positive tail shape \code{xi}. Use
\code{~ 1} to estimate a constant parameter. Use separate coefficient blocks.
All three predictors can include offsets. Responses must be finite
numeric vectors. Finite nonnegative case weights multiply log likelihood
contributions, with at least one positive weight.

The blend boundaries are the \code{pa} and \code{pb} quantiles of the positive-shape
GEV. A Gumbel distribution matches its CDF at both points. The response
CDF uses the Gumbel to the left, the GEV to the right, and interpolates
the log CDFs with a Beta(5, 5) CDF weight between the boundaries. The
density is the derivative of this CDF. Matching at both ends gives
continuous log-density derivatives through order four. Analytic
predictor derivatives retain the Gumbel limit as shape tends to zero.
A shape near zero can be weakly identified. Extreme predictors or a
blend width below the resolution of the response can cause numerical
failure. The shape has no upper bound.

In \code{\link[mgcv:gam]{mgcv::gam()}}, REML uses outer Newton, outer BFGS or extended
Fellner--Schall (\code{optimizer = "efs"}). EFS uses \code{gamma = 1} internally
and requires all smoothing parameters free and unlinked, or all fixed.
mgcv passes an unset objective scale to the outer \code{nlm} and \code{optim}
optimizers for general families, so those optimizers are unavailable.
Use outer Newton or BFGS when EFS is unsuitable.
NCV and QNCV use outer BFGS with default leave-one-out or explicit
\code{nei} neighbourhoods. They use a one-step approximation to deletion,
and QNCV evaluates a quadratic approximation to the held-out log
likelihood. Supplying \code{nei} with REML also selects outer BFGS.
With mgcv 1.9-3 and 1.9-4, upstream jackknife reparameterization fails
for fully unpenalized NCV/QNCV. Supply \code{nei} with \code{jackknife = FALSE}
to evaluate the criterion without computing jackknife covariance.

Shape-constrained models in \code{mgcv::scasm()} were verified on mgcv 1.9-4 and 1.9-5
with REML/EFS and a monotone smooth with binding inequalities. Its
fitter hardwires REML/EFS and provides no NCV, QNCV, Newton or BFGS
smoothing-selection route. Omit \code{method}, \code{optimizer} and \code{nei} from
direct \code{scasm()} calls. In mgcv 1.9-5 the upstream fitter can fail when
all coefficient directions of a smooth are constrained, or when no
smooth is supplied. \code{scasm()} is unavailable in mgcv 1.9-3.
\code{bam()}, \code{gamm()} and other criteria are outside the supported scope.

Fitted values and response predictions contain \code{location}, \code{spread},
and \code{shape}, in that order. mgcv can drop response prediction column
names. Link predictions return location, log spread and log shape.
Response prediction standard errors use the delta method and describe
parameter estimation uncertainty.

Use the three-column response-scale parameter matrix \code{mu} with
\code{rd(mu, wt = 1, scale = 1)},
\code{cdf(q, mu, wt = 1, scale = 1, logp = FALSE, lower.tail = TRUE)} and
\code{qf(p, mu, wt = 1, scale = 1, lower.tail = TRUE, log.p = FALSE)}.
Supply a scalar or one value per row for \code{q} or \code{p}. Missing inputs
propagate. These callbacks ignore weights and external scale and accept
shape zero for the limiting Gumbel law. Quantiles are evaluated in closed
form in both tails and by bracketed inversion in the blend.

Native \code{gratia_diagnostics()} provides probabilities, quantiles, simulation
and the complete response moments. Mean exists for \code{xi < 1} and is
reported as \code{NA} otherwise. Variance exists for \code{xi < 0.5} and is
reported as \code{Inf} otherwise. Response and Pearson residuals require
their corresponding moments. Deviance and null deviance are undefined.
Use PIT or quantile residuals when moments are unavailable.
